.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1212 PM PDT Tue Oct 6 2026
(This evening through Wednesday night)

Patches of fog and stratus linger at the immediate coast, associated
with a shallow marine layer that sits around 500 feet deep. The rest
of the region remains clear as large scale ridging results in
sinking air, thus suppressing cloud formation processes. The ridging
is also helping to compress the marine layer, so don`t expect any
overnight stratus outside of the immediate coast and favored areas
adjacent to it, including the Petaluma Gap, the Golden Gate, and the
coastal plain around Monterey Bay. On the flip side, for higher
elevation areas above around 1000 feet, don`t expect that much
relief at night. The marine layer inversion has set up right along
those areas, resulting in a thermal belting effect where overnight
lows remain in the upper 60s to middle 70s, limiting the ability of
the body to recover from the heat of the day. As a final note, as
the ridge has set up right over our area, local pressure gradients
are relatively low, and the onshore flow this afternoon has likewise
been neutered.

Over the last couple of days, the default model blend has been
running warmer than the observed temperatures across much of the
region. On a broad scale, this is relatively minor (around 3 degrees
warmer in the forecast across the inland valleys) so not a
significant deviation to correct for. At the immediate coast,
temperatures are significantly cooler than the model blend output
would otherwise suggest. For example, downtown San Francisco
reported a high of 71 yesterday compared to a forecast high of 80.
Based off that assessment, I have lowered the high temperatures for
today and Wednesday at the immediate coastal regions by around 5
degrees, into the middle 60s to lower 70s. This might still be an
overshoot in some patches of the coast where the stratus continues
to linger well into the afternoon. Elsewhere, high temperatures
remain in the 80s to the lower 90s near the Bays, while the interior
valleys should expect highs in the middle 90s to the triple digits
(near 105 in the warmest spots of interior Monterey County).

A Heat Advisory remains in effect for the interior regions through
the end of Thursday, excluding the coastal strip and the northern
Salinas Valley, as widespread Moderate HeatRisk blankets the
interior, corresponding to a moderate risk of heat-related illnesses
for sensitive populations, including children, the elderly, pregnant
women, people with chronic illnesses, and people who work or live
outdoors without access to adequate cooling or hydration. In
addition, many of the higher elevations are seeing Major HeatRisk,
corresponding to a high risk for heat-related illnesses for anyone
without hydration or cooling. Follow these tips to avoid heat
exhaustion and heatstroke:

* Stay hydrated and drink plenty of fluids.
* Wear lightweight, light-colored clothing.
* Reduce time spent outdoors or stay in the shade.
* Never leave people or pets unattended in vehicles.
* Use sunscreen if going to the coast or the pool.

Minor coastal flooding is expected at the Bayshore locations along
the San Pablo Bay through the next few days as up to 0.9 ft of
thermal expansion surge significantly impacts the tidal level. At
the San Francisco tidal gauge, high tide is expected to be 0.6 ft
above normal (6.48 ft MLLW) at 8:58 PM Tuesday 10/6 and 0.6 ft above
normal (6.44 ft MLLW) at 10:19 AM Wednesday 10/7. Confidence in
these specific values is moderate and the high tide yesterday
evening ran to 0.8 ft above normal (6.64 ft MLLW) representing a
surge of around 1.0 feet.

