.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1212 PM PDT Tue Oct 6 2026
(Thursday through next Monday)

Hot and dry conditions continue through Thursday with the Heat
Advisory for the inland locations expiring at 10 PM that night. The
first hints of a pattern change arrive on Friday, when an upper
level trough forming over British Columbia and the Pacific
Northwest, a decaying husk of an upper level low coming in from the
Central Pacific, and a weakening Hurricane Rachel (expected to be a
Tropical Storm by Friday) will interact to flatten the upper level
ridge and bring troughing across the West Coast for the upcoming
weekend. As a side note, with the breakdown of the ridge on Friday,
confidence in the  High temperatures will drop closer to, or
slightly below, the seasonal averages by the weekend, with highs in
the 70s across the inland valleys and the 60s at the Pacific coast
and near the Bays. Ensemble model cluster analysis is coming around
to some sort of trough across the region for the weekend, and
continues to hold on to chances for something resembling an inside
slider pattern towards the end of the weekend into the early part of
next week, although confidence is not high. As the trough deepens,
various clusters show the trough axis developing somewhere from
central Oregon and Northern California to the Great Basin. Many of
the scenarios support some form of northerly flow over the Bay Area
and Central Coast, but the exact orientation of the low will
determine whether the flow retains a more westerly component or
pushes more from due north or the northeast.

Minor coastal flooding continues along the bayshore San Pablo Bay on
Thursday with high tide at San Francisco expected to be 0.8 ft above
normal (6.68 ft MLLW) at 10:51 AM Thursday 10/8. With higher
astronomical tides expected Friday into the weekend, a Coastal Flood
Advisory may need to be issued for bayshore locations along the San
Francisco and Monterey Bays closer to that time. In addition,
southerly swell from Rachel will increase the risk of rip currents
and sneaker waves later this week into the weekend as long period
swell of 15 to 18 seconds arrives.

